        "create_time": 1778573456.853,
        "update_time": 1778573481.604851,
        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "rate normalization → SBRAD (hybrid protocol): For\r\nSBRAD-positive samples (n = 1,190) and matched nega\r\ntive controls (n = 1,190), each heartbeat was time-warped\r\nto a target rate of 75 bpm. This preserves per-beat QRS\r\nmorphology while eliminating slow-rate information, testing\r\nwhether SBRAD classification depends specifically on low\r\nventricular rate. The target perturbation operator is global\r\ntime-warping (not window-based), because rate is a whole\r\nsignal property rather than a local waveform feature. Three\r\nmatched anatomical controls use linear interpolation (160ms\r\nwindow, qualified operator): QRS complex [R-peak ± 80ms],\r\nST segment [R-peak + 40ms to R-peak + 200ms], and\r\nrandom non-QRS window (160ms, 5 random placements per\r\nexam with logit averaging). This is a hybrid protocol: the\r\ntarget perturbation is rate normalization, while all controls use\r\nlinear interpolation—the qualified operator established by the\r\nvalidity battery. The same protocol was applied to all three\r\nraw-signal architectures (ResNet, SE-ResNet, Transformer)\r\nwith identical matched positive/negative subsets and patient\r\nlevel bootstrap CIs (1,000 iterations). The fraction of SBRAD\r\npositive exams with HR change ≥ 1 bpm was recorded to\r\nconfirm effective perturbation delivery.\n\n\n# 这里的操作怎么理解"
          ]
        },
